@@ -0,0 +1,27 @@
+# Feature Specification: createPatch
+
+**Input**: "Add `createPatch(from, to)`: a minimal RFC 6902 JSON Patch turning `from` into `to`."
+
+Standards: JSON Patch (RFC 6902 §4 operations, §5 ordered application), JSON Pointer (RFC 6901 §3 escaping, §4 evaluation), JSON (RFC 8259).
+
+## User Story 1 - Send only what changed (P1)
+A client sends a patch instead of the new document.
+- Applying `createPatch(from, to)` to `from` with `applyPatch` yields a value equal to `to`.
+- Equal documents give `[]`. Differing root types/primitives give one `replace` at `""`.
+- Objects: removed keys -> `remove`, new keys -> `add`, changed keys -> recursion. Nothing is emitted for unchanged parts.
+
+## User Story 2 - Arrays stay minimal (P1)
+- An inserted/deleted item gives a single `add`/`remove`; neighbours are not rewritten.
+- A moved item gives a `move`, or a `remove` plus an `add`; items in between are untouched.
+- Changed items inside arrays are patched in place by recursion.
+
+## Requirements
+- FR-1 Export `createPatch(from, to): PatchOperation[]` from `src/index.ts`, doc comment citing RFC 6902.
+- FR-2 Inputs are never mutated; the patch shares no structure with them.
+- FR-3 Pointer tokens escape `~` as `~0` and `/` as `~1` (RFC 6901 §3); keys like `__proto__` are handled safely.
+- FR-4 `null` and a missing member are distinct; empty `{}`/`[]` are values.
+- FR-5 Only `add`, `remove`, `replace`, `move` operations are used; `remove` of `""` is never emitted.
+
+## Assumptions
+- Inputs are JSON values. Array element identity is deep equality. Member order in objects is irrelevant.
+- Minimality is per-change, not a globally provable minimum.
